Path('/app/answer.json').write_text('{"answer": "The wrapper function that defers hash algorithm access until execution is `_lazy_sha1`, defined in `src/flask/sessions.py` at lines 290-295. It implements lazy initialization by wrapping the `hashlib.sha1()` call inside a function body rather than accessing it at module import time.\\n\\nThe function is assigned as a `staticmethod` to the `digest_method` attribute of the `SecureCookieSessionInterface` class (line 307). When Flask imports the sessions module, `_lazy_sha1` is defined but not executed\\u2014the actual `hashlib.sha1()` call only happens when the function is invoked at runtime, specifically when `get_signing_serializer()` creates a `URLSafeTimedSerializer` instance (lines 317-335).\\n\\nThis deferral mechanism delays cryptographic availability validation because:\\n1. **Import time**: Only the function definition is evaluated; no hash algorithm access occurs, so no validation happens.\\n2. **Runtime**: When a session needs to be signed/verified, `get_signing_serializer()` is called, which passes `self.digest_method` (the `_lazy_sha1` function) to `URLSafeTimedSerializer`, which then calls it, triggering the `hashlib.sha1()` access.\\n\\nIn FIPS-compliant builds where SHA-1 may not be available, accessing `hashlib.sha1` at import time would immediately raise an exception, preventing the module from loading. By deferring access until runtime, developers can:\\n- Successfully import Flask even in restricted environments\\n- Configure an alternative digest method (e.g., SHA-256) by overriding `digest_method` before sessions are actually used\\n- Handle the error gracefully at runtime if needed, rather than failing at import time\\n\\nThis pattern is documented in the function\'s docstring (lines 291-293) and was introduced to address issue #5448, as noted in `CHANGES.rst` (lines 62-64)."}' + '\n')
